so as I mentioned we're going to  describe here how a virus which  symbolizes an innovation that in this  case the virus is HIV comes into a much  more complex system that has evolved to  keep itself the same to maintain status  quo and start to change that system and  become part of the DNA so how does it do  that well let's go with the first step  so cells when they are communicating  with their environment they have things  on their surface that are called  receptors and these actually transit the  boundary and help the cell read and  understand things that are going on  outside these have a pre-existing  function completely independent from  what the virus does but the virus has  evolved to take advantage of these  boundary spanning receptors so H Ivy's  first step is to bind to a pre-existing  receptor that trans it's the boundary of  the cell then it binds to another one  and when it does that the virus is able  to fuse with the surface of the cell and  introduce its component parts so it has  viral proteins it has viral materials  these come into the cell because it  bound to a pre-existing receptor so  that's step one of our learning from HIV  in bringing innovations into an existing  system once it has done that as I  mentioned it introduces its viral  material this viral material is RNA now  as I said in the first video inside of  human cells we have DNA the human cells  don't like RNA for the purposes of  understanding the message HIV has  evolved to recognize that so it changes  its RNA into DNA and it uses its own  viral proteins to make that happen  so it's making its message accessible to  the larger system that it's changing so  that's part of step to make the message  accessible another thing that happens as  HIV changes its RNA into DNA is that it  introduces lots of little changes into  the DNA itself these are mutations and  this makes HIV incredibly adaptable this  is part of the reason why HIV is so good  at spreading and it's so hard for us to  stop it our lesson from for this is that  the message of the innovation needs to  be adaptable so let me just pause here  for a second and say what can we learn  from this this is all very interesting  but what can we learn when we're  thinking about introducing innovations  into human systems so let's take one  specific examples let's imagine now that  this complex system into which we're  introducing something new is a hospital  and this is a real world example  hospitals have to deal obviously with  all different kinds of challenges but  one specific challenge that we've  studied in our team is the patients with  a certain kind of heart attack who come  to the door of the hospital and they  need a certain kind of treatment within  90 minutes so the idea is that these  patients arrive at the door of the  hospital they need a treatment that's  called a balloon within 90 minutes it  used to be that many hospitals in the  United States were having  door-to-balloon times that were much  longer than 90 minutes once that change  is introduced and the patient's get  treatment faster they have much better  health outcomes and much greater  likelihood of of success in and  sometimes it makes a difference for  survival so the innovation in this case  would be introducing changes to the way  the hospital functions so that door to  balloon time is lowered to 90 minutes or  less so how did this Howard this  innovation actually introduced well  first it was brought in by people who  were already boundary spanners so it may  be a cardiologist who had read the  papers in peer-reviewed literature about  reducing door-to-balloon time sometimes  it was a quality improvement officer  whose job inside of the hospital is to  bring a new idea  to help improve processes but always the  innovation came in on a pre-existing  boundary spanner and then the next piece  is to make the message accessible so  sticking with the door to balloon time  example the steps of reducing time from  door to balloon were originally as i  mentioned published in academic medical  journals but those steps needed to be  accessible and understandable to the  people inside of the hospital who are  actually doing patient care so that's  making the message accessible making it  understandable to the people who are  going to be using it and also the  innovators for door-to-balloon time  found that they also had to be flexible  they had to say okay here are the steps  that you can follow to reduce  door-to-balloon time and sometimes  people would say well we can't we can't  do step 3 quite like that okay then  let's change it you're going to keep  some of the core message the same but  you're going to be flexible and  adaptable so that this process will  proceed so from this biological model  have spread something that's evolved to  be very good at spreading we can take  these two lessons about how to introduce  an innovation health innovation into a  complex human system now this is not the  completion of the lifecycle i keep kind  of talking about getting into the DNA  and that's really where HIV is going so  in the next video i'll take you through  the steps we'll put them here about how  HIV actually gets into the DNA of a cell  and that's really the goal that's the  implementation of this change to make it  a permanent part of the system
 

